523:
The purpose of this chapter, pertaining to land clearing and tree protection is to:
(1) Lessen air pollution and carbon dioxide levels in the air, and promote clean air quality by increasing dust filtration;
(2) Reduce noise, heat, and glare;
(3) Prevent soil erosion; prevent rivers, canals, ditches, and other waterways from silting; prevent reductions in the drainage holding capacity of land; improve surface drainage and minimize flooding;
(4) Minimize increases in temperatures on lands with natural and planted tree cover;
(5) Maintain moisture levels in the air of lands with natural tree cover;
(6) Reduce noise, heat and glare, and ensure that these and other distractions of movement in one area do not adversely affect activity within other adjacent areas;
(7) Emphasize the importance of trees and vegetation as both a visual and physical buffer;
(8) Protect and enhance the aesthetic qualities of the community to ensure that tree removal does not reduce property values;
(9) Minimize the cost of construction and maintenance of drainage systems necessitated by the increased flow and diversion of surface waters;
(10) Maintain, wherever possible, a minimum of 50 percent canopy cover across the city.
(Ord. No. 99-12-01, § III, 12-14-1999)

559:
The following uses or conditions shall be and are hereby prohibited within wellhead protection zones, whether or not such use or condition may otherwise be ordinarily included as a part of a use permitted under section 121-4:
(1) Surface use or storage of hazardous material, expressly including commercial use of agricultural pesticides;
(2) Septic tanks or drain fields appurtenant thereto;
(3) Impervious surfaces other than roofs of buildings, and streets, driveways and walks serving buildings permitted under section 121-4;
(4) Sanitary landfills;
(5) Hazardous waste disposal sites;
(6) Stormwater infiltration basins;
(7) Underground storage tanks;
(8) Sanitary sewer lines within 150 feet of a wellhead.
(Ord. No. 98-11-1, § 5, 11-24-1998)
